# Heterostructured Bimetallic MOF‐on‐MOF Architectures for Efficient Oxygen Evolution Reaction

## abstract
Electron modulation presents a captivating approach to fabricate efficient electrocatalysts for the oxygen evolution reaction (OER), yet it remains a challenging undertaking. In this study, an effective strategy is proposed to regulate the electronic structure of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) by the construction of MOF-on-MOF heterogeneous architectures. As a representative heterogeneous architectures, MOF-74 on MOF-274 hybrids are in situ prepared on 3D metal substrates (NiFe alloy foam (NFF)) via a two-step self-assembly method, resulting in MOF-(74 + 274)@NFF. Through a combination of spectroscopic and theory calculation, the successful modulation of the electronic property of MOF-(74 + 274)@NFF is unveiled. This modulation arises from the phase conjugation of the two MOFs and the synergistic effect of the multimetallic centers (Ni and Fe). Consequently, MOF-(74 + 274)@NFF exhibits excellent OER activity, displaying ultralow overpotentials of 198 and 223 mV at a current density of 10 mA cm-2 in the 1.0 and 0.1 M KOH solutions, respectively. This work paves the way for manipulating the electronic structure of electrocatalysts to enhance their catalytic activity.

Electron modulation presents a captivating approach to fabricate efficient electrocatalysts for the oxygen evolution reaction (OER), yet it remains a challenging undertaking. In this study, an effective strategy is proposed to regulate the electronic structure of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) by the construction of MOF-on-MOF heterogeneous architectures. As a representative heterogeneous architectures, MOF-74 on MOF-274 hybrids are in situ prepared on 3D metal substrates (NiFe alloy foam (NFF)) via a two-step self-assembly method, resulting in MOF- $(74+274)@{\mathsf{N F F}}$ . Through a combination of spectroscopic and theory calculation, the successful modulation of the electronic property of MOF- $(74+274)\textcircled{<}\mathsf{N F F}$ is unveiled. This modulation arises from the phase conjugation of the two MOFs and the synergistic effect of the multimetallic centers (Ni and Fe). Consequently, MOF- $(74+274)\textcircled{<}\mathsf{N F F}$ exhibits excellent OER activity, displaying ultralow overpotentials of 198 and $223~\mathsf{m V}$ at a current density of $10m A c m^{-2}$ in the 1.0 and 0.1 M KOH solutions, respectively. This work paves the way for manipulating the electronic structure of electrocatalysts to enhance their catalytic activity.
## 1. Introduction
Among various alternative energy resources, hydrogen fuel holds significant potential for substituting fossil fuel, owing to its high energy density $(140~\mathrm{~M}\mathrm{~\kg^{-1}})$ and zero carbon emission.[1] Electrochemical water splitting is currently recognized as a promising alternative for hydrogen production.[2] However, the sluggish kinetics of oxygen evolution reaction (OER) substantially impede the energy conversion efficiency, a consequence of the four-step proton-coupled electron transfer process.[3] While noble-metal-based materials like $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ have been the go-to choices for facilitating efficient OER, their limited availability restricts broader application.[4] Therefore, exploring non-noble metal electrocatalysts with efficient activity and high stability is essential.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) hold significant promise in the field of electrocatalysis, owing to their rich porosity, high surface areas, adjustable structures, and abundant active sites.[5] Furthermore, the electronic structures of MOFs can be precisely manipulated by tuning metal centers and organic ligands, which is crucial since electronic structure significantly impacts OER performance by affecting the binding energy between electrocatalysts and reaction intermediates in the process.[6] For example, Liu and co-workers adopted a lattice-strained strategy, enabling partial electron transfer from Ni to neighboring O atoms to regulate the electronic structures of MOFs. The resulting material achieved an overpotential of ${\approx}210~\mathrm{mV}$ at a current density of $200~\mathrm{{mA}}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .[7] Beyond that, the construction of multimetallic MOFs offers an alternative way for the electronic structure modulation. Notably, NiFe-MOF showcases subtle electronic structure adjustments, owing to the synergetic effect between Ni and Fe ions, resulting in enhanced catalytic activity and establishing it as one of the most efficient OER electrocatalysts.[8]
In addition, construction of dual-MOF hybrids has emerged to be an effective strategy for fine-tuning the electronic structure of MOFs, thereby enhancing their catalytic activity.[9] By combining two distinct MOF structures into a hybrid catalyst, it becomes possible to retain their individual characteristics while also harnessing advantages that a single MOF cannot provide. To date, several conjugated MOF-based materials have been developed as electrocatalysts, demonstrating excellent OER performance.[10] Nevertheless, the intrinsic electron transfer mechanism of MOFon-MOF hybrids remains elusive and intriguing, yet pivotal for designing highly efficient OER catalysts.
In this study, we rationally designed a bimetallic MOF-onMOF heterogeneous structure to achieve precise charge modulation by the electron transfer between MOF hybrids and synergistic effect of bimetallic centers, resulting in a significant enhancement in catalytic performance. Specifically, via a twostep solvothermal epitaxial growth process, we grew MOF-74, which features a similar structure with MOF-274, on the surface of MOF-274, yielding hybrids referred to as MOF- $(74~+$ 274) $@$ NFF.[11] Wherein, the NiFe alloy foam (NFF) served a dual function as a metal source and a substrate to support MOFs, reducing contact resistance between catalysts and electrode, while exposing more active sites.[12] Significantly, the resulting heterogeneous interfaces between MOF-274 and MOF-74 facilitated electron transfer, enriched active sites, and modulated the electronic structure, leading to superior electrocatalytic performance, outperforming single MOFs. Detailed investigation and theoretical calculations revealed a synergistic effect between Ni and Fe ions, along with partial charge transfer from MOF-274 to MOF-74, modulating the electronic structure of MOF- $(74+$ 274) $\boldsymbol{\@}\textcircled{a}\mathrm{NFF}$ . This modification significantly decreased the energy barrier of rate-determining step (RDS), resulting in the enhanced catalytic activity and lowering the OER activation energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ .
## 2. Results and Discussions
## 2.1. Synthesis and Characterization of Electrocatalysts
A facile stepwise solvothermal process was carried out for fabricating MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ , as illustrated in Figure 1a (nomenclature and preparation of the materials are described in the “Experimental Section” and in Table S1 in the Supporting Information). Ni and Fe ions tend to be released from the NFF surface in the mixed solution (N,N-Dimethylformamide (DMF), EtOH, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and $0.02\mathrm{~M~HCl}$ ) at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , and coordinated with $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dihydroxy- $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -diphthalic acid $(\mathrm{H_{4}D O B P D C})$ to generate MOF-274 on NFF. Further, the obtained $\mathsf{M O F-}274@\mathsf{N F F}$ was immersed into a secondary growth solution containing DMF, EtOH, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid $\mathrm{'H_{4}D O B D C)}$ . Then, the Ni and Fe ions were persistently released from the
NFF, consequently yielding MOF- $(74~+~274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ heterogeneous architectures, with MOF-74 growing on the surface of MOF-274. It is noteworthy that MOF-274 exhibits unit cell parameters $(a=21.7036\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $b=21.7036\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $c=6.8440\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $\alpha=$ $90.00^{\circ}$ , $\beta=90.00^{\circ}$ , and $\gamma=120.00^{\circ})$ which are quite similar to those of MOF-74 $(a=25.3861\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , $b=25.3861\mathrm{~\AA}$ , $c=6.4625\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $\alpha=$ $90.00^{\circ}$ , $\beta=90.00^{\circ}$ , and $\gamma=120.00^{\circ})$ . Given their analogous lattice topologies, hybridization of MOF-274 and MOF-74 can be enabled by the epitaxial growth method, creating the MOF-on-MOF heterostructure. Taking the synthesis of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ as an example, during the second step of the hydrothermal synthesis process, noticeable transformation occurs. After 30 min of the reaction, the surface of MOF-274 gradually develops a slight roughness. Subsequently, over the course of a $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ reaction, numerous protrusions densely cluster on the MOF-274 surface. After $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the rods’ surface becomes smoother, and their width increases, indicative of MOF-74 growth on the MOF-274 surface, indicating the formation of hybrid MOFs (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The crystalline structure of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ was determined by X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis. The XRD pattern of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ featured characteristic diffraction peaks of both MOF-274 $(4.68^{\circ}$ and $8.10^{\circ}$ ) and MOF-74 $(6.72^{\circ}$ and $11.76^{\circ})$ , respectively, confirming the in situ growth of the MOF hybrids (Figure 1b).[13] The peaks at $41.26^{\circ}$ , $51.58^{\circ}$ , and $76.00^{\circ}$ are ascribed to the NFF phase, pertaining to (111), (200), and (220) crystal planes of $\mathrm{FeNi}_{3}$ (JCPDS No. 38–0419), respectively. Moreover, the growth of MOF-274, MOF-74, and MOF- $(74+274)$ on different substrates (NFF, Ni foam (NF), andFe foam (FF)) was tracked using XRD (Figure S2, Supporting Information), verifying the viability of self-supported growth for both single-MOF and MOF hybrids on diverse substrates. According to the Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra of MOFs on NFF shown in Figure 1c, no obvious signals of substrates are detected. The absence of a band in $1650{-}1660~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ region confirms the coordination of ligand functional groups $\mathrm{(H_{4}D O B P D C}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DOBDC})$ with the metal ions. The notable peaks in 1400–1460 and $800{-}870~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ regions are assigned to the stretching vibration of $\nu({\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}})$ and $\nu(\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H})$ , and the peak around $1541~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponds to the asymmetric stretching vibration of $\nu_{\mathrm{as}}(\mathrm{COO^{-}})$ . The above FTIR peaks are captured in the spectra of all MOF-based materials as a common feature, which is in conformity with the XRD results, confirming the successful growth of MOF hybrids on metal foams (Figure 1c; Figure S3, Supporting Information). To determine the ratio of each MOF in the MOF hybrids, MOF powders were peeled off from the substrates and measured to $^1\mathrm{H}$ -NMR. The ratios of MOF-274 versus MOF-74 are 1:0.38, 1:0.39, and 1:0.35 for self-supported MOF$(74+274)$ on NFF, FF, and NF, respectively (Figures S4–S6, Supporting Information), demonstrating close similarities among them. To investigate whether the coordinated linkers of MOF-274 were replaced by $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ during the secondary solvothermal process, we prepared NiFe $(74+274)$ via a two-step process, which was similar to the fabrication of MOF- $(74~+$ 274)@NFF, with the exception that metal foam was replaced by metal salts during the first solvothermal step and no additional metal source was introduced during the secondary growth step. The $^1\mathrm{H}$ -NMR spectrum of NiFe $(74+274)$ displays chemical shifts at 7.03–7.05, 7.77–7.80, and $7.95\mathrm{\ppm}$ , in accordance with MOF-274 (Figure S7, Supporting Information). No signals related to MOF-74 are observed, suggesting that $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ is incapable of displacing the coordinated linkers of MOF-274 in the second step, thereby proving the absence of MOF with mixed linkers $\mathrm{(H_{4}D O B P D C}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DOBDC})$ in MOF- $(74+274)$ .
The morphologies of MOFs on the metal foam substrates were examined using field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM). The MOFs $@$ NFF/NF/FF series (MOF- $(74\:+\:274)$ ), MOF-274, and MOF-74) with rod-like morphologies display uniform growth on metal foams (Figure 2; Figures S8–S10, Supporting Information). Notably, the rod widths of MOF- $(74~+$ $274)\textcircled{a}\mathrm{NFF/NF/FF}$ are 17, 20, and $29\ \mathrm{nm}$ thicker than those of $\mathsf{M O F-}274@\mathrm{NFF/NF/FF}$ , respectively (Figure 2; Figures S8 and S9, Supporting Information), indicating secondary growth contributed from the MOF-74, while preserving the overall morphologies of MOF-274. The energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) analysis showed an atomic ratio of Ni versus Fe of ${\approx}1{:}1$ in MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ , revealing a different metal ratio compared to NFF (Ni:Fe/7:3) (Figure S11, Supporting Information). The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images confirmed the rod-like structure of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF/NF/FF}$ and provided detailed insights into the hybridization of MOF-274 and MOF-74 (Figure $2\mathrm{e}$ ; Figures S12 and S13, Supporting Information). We further performed high-resolution TEM (HRTEM)
to investigate the heterostructure using low-dose HRTEM technique. As shown in the HRTEM image, the characteristic lattice fringes of 1.8 and $1.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ are indexed to the (010) crystal plane of MOF-274 and the (110) crystal plane of MOF-74, respectively, affirming the preservation of the crystallinity after the second growth of MOF-74 (Figure 2f). This was further corroborated by the selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern (Figure 2g). These characterizations indicated the successful synthesis of the MOF-on-MOF-based core–shell heterogeneous catalysts. Particularly, the MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ rod exhibits a rough surface, anticipated to provide more exposed active sites (Figure 2e). According to Table S3 (Supporting Information), the elemental ratio of Ni to Fe obtained from the corresponding EDS mapping is ${\approx}1{:}1$ , consistent with the results of SEM-EDS. The element mapping reveals that all elements (Ni/Fe, C, and O) are homogeneously distributed in the MOF-on-MOF hybrids (Figure 2h; Figures S12 and S13, Supporting Information). The desolvated MOFs exhibited type-I $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms with high adsorbed volumes (Figure 1d; Figure S14, Supporting Information), indicative of their high Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas (Table S2, Supporting Information). The larger surface area of MOF-274 compared to MOF-74 can be attributed to its longer linker, offering more available space $(\mathrm{DOBPDC^{4-}}>\mathrm{DOBDC^{4-}};$ .[14] Within the same series of metal sources, the BET surface area and $\Nu_{2}$ uptake capacity of MOF- $(74+274)$ fall between those of MOF274 and MOF-74. Furthermore, MOF- $(74+274)$ possesses the characteristic pore structures of MOF-274 and MOF-74 (Figure S15, Supporting Information). This experiment evidence confirms that the MOF-on-MOF hybrids integrate the textural properties of the respective single MOF.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was conducted to investigate the composition and electronic states of MOFs. The MOFs ${\mathfrak{Q}}\mathrm{NF/FF}$ series were prepared for comparison with MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ . The XPS survey scan spectra reveal the presence of Ni/Fe, C, and O elements in the MOFs on metal foams (Figure S16, Supporting Information). In the C 1s regions of the MOFs $@$ NFF/NF/FF series, three peaks of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ , ${\mathrm{C-C}}{\mathrm{=}}0$ , and $\mathtt{C-O}$ are observed, corresponding to benzoic rings of the organic ligand, carboxylate group, and hydroxyl group, respectively (Figures S17–S19, Supporting Information).[15] The high-resolution O 1s spectra can be assigned to M─O, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{O=C-C}}$ , and $\mathtt{C-O}$ (Figures S17–S19, Supporting Information).[16] As shown in Figure 3a, two spin–orbit doublets attributed to $\mathrm{Ni}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2\mathtt{p}_{1/2}$ are observed. For the $\mathsf{M O F s@N F}$ series, Ni $2\mathtt{p}_{3/2}$ at the binding energies of 856.06, 856.20, and $855.89\mathrm{eV}$ is detected for MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NF}$ , $\mathsf{M O F-}274@\mathsf{N F}$ , and $\mathsf{M O F-}74@\mathsf{N F}$ , respectively, verifying the presence of $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ . Noteworthy that the binding energy of Ni $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ for MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NF}$ decreases by $0.14~\mathrm{eV}$ compared to $\mathsf{M O F-}274@\mathsf{N F}$ , while higher than that of $\mathsf{M O F-}74@\mathsf{N F}$ , suggesting that the electron transfers from MOF-274 to MOF-74 upon the formation of MOF hybrids. A similar phenomenon is observed in the Fe XPS spectra for the
MOFs $@$ FF series. The Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ spectra can be deconvoluted into two peaks representing $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ species, indicating that the coexistence of two valence states of Fe in Fe-MOFs. In the Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ orbits, MOF- $(74+274)\textcircled{a}1$ FF exhibits lower binding energy $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ : $710.79\mathrm{eV}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ : $713.27\mathrm{eV}$ ) compared to MOF-274@FF $(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+};711.16\mathrm{eV}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ : $713.65\mathrm{eV}_{\it$ ). In contrast, compared with MOF-74 $@$ FF $\mathrm{(Fe^{2+};710.69~e V}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ : $712.90\mathrm{eV}.$ ), MOF- $(74+$ $274)@\mathrm{FF}$ exhibits higher binding energy (Figure 3b), reflecting the electron reconfiguration of the hybrid MOFs. To further illustrate the electron-transfer phenomenon between MOF-274 and MOF-74, we conducted a detailed comparison of the binding energies of Ni $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ and Fe $2\mathtt{p}_{3/2}$ orbitals between MOF-274@NFF and MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ . For $\mathsf{N i}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ orbit, the binding energy for $\mathsf{M O F-}274@\mathsf{N F F}$ is $856.18\mathrm{eV}.$ However, when MOF-74 is in situ grown onto the surface of MOF-274@NFF to form MOF$(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ , the binding energy of Ni $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ for the hybrids decreases to $855.90\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure 3c). Similarly, the binding energy of Fe $2\mathtt{p}_{3/2}$ for MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ $\mathrm{(Fe^{2+}{:}711.25~e V}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ : $713.95\mathrm{eV})$ is lower than that of MOF-274@NFF $(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ : $711.51\mathrm{eV}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ : $715.20\mathrm{eV}_{\it\cdot}$ ) (Figure 3d). The strategy of fine-tuning the electronic structure of MOFs allows for the functionalization of the initial single MOF, thereby optimizing the Gibbs free energy of the adsorbed intermediates during the OER process.
These analyses confirm that precise adjustments to the electronic structure of MOFs can be achieved through the integration of different MOFs. Additionally, previous studies have highlighted that NiFe-based electrocatalysts exhibited promising catalytic activity owing to the partial electron transfer between Ni and Fe, thereby modifying the electron properties of the active centers.[17] Furthermore, the integration of Fe and Ni can reduce energy consumption during the OER process.[18] For MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ , the binding energy of Ni $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ is slightly lower $(0.16~\mathrm{eV})$ than that of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NF}$ (Figure 3c). Conversely, MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ shows an opposite trend with Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ , where the binding energies of Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ in MOF-(74 $+\ 274)\textcircled{a}\mathrm{NFF}$ ( $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ : 711.25 eV and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ : 713.95 eV) exhibit a positive shift compared with that of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{FF}$ (Figure 3d). These results suggest electron transfer from Fe to Ni in MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ , which can modulate the electron environment of active sites for boosting OER activity.[19] Consequently, the synergistic effect of hybrid MOFs and the associated pulling effect between multimetallic centers optimize the electronic structure of active sites, resulting in superior OER performance. Furthermore, the valence band maximum energy of MOF hybrids is closer to the Fermi level than that of single MOFs, facilitating faster charge transfer (Figure S20, Supporting Information).[20]
X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) was employed to scrutinize the local electron structure and atomic configuration of Ni and Fe species in MOFs. As depicted in Figure 3e, the X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectra of the Ni K-edge of $\mathsf{M O F s@N F/N F F}$ show that the Ni profiles closely resemble that of NiO, signifying that the Ni species are in the oxidation state of $+2$ . Notably, the XANES spectra of Ni $K$ -edge for MOFs exhibit a slight redshift compared to that of NiO, indicative of the valence state of Ni in MOFs as $+(2-\delta)$ . Moreover, the Ni K-edge position in MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NF}$ shifts to lower and higher energy in comparison to $\mathsf{M O F-}274@\mathsf{N F}$ and $\mathsf{M O F-}74@\mathsf{N F}$ , respectively, indicating that its valence state of Ni lies between that of MOF$274@\mathrm{NF}$ and $\mathsf{M O F-}74@\mathsf{N F}$ . The Fe $K$ -edge XANES spectra of MOFs $@$ FF/NFF exhibit higher energy levels than FeO and nearly overlap with $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , implying an average valence state of Fe as $+3$ (Figure 3f). The Fe K-edge spectrum of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ displays higher energy compared to $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , suggesting that the Fe species exist as $\mathrm{Fe}^{(3+\delta)+}$ , attributed to the electron reconstruction in MOFs (partial electron transfer from Fe to Ni). Previous research has demonstrated that the higher valence state of Fe is advantageous in electrocatalytic water splitting.[21] The energy absorption edge position of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{FF}$ is higher than that of MOF-74@FF, but lower than that of $\mathrm{MOF}{-}274@\mathrm{FF}.$ demonstrating that the valence state of Fe in MOF hybrids is in between those of the two individual MOFs. The XANES results are consistent with XPS, affirming the occurrence of electronic reconstruction in MOF-on-MOF hybrids due to the electron transfer between MOF-274 and MOF-74, which can finely tune the electronic structure of active sites in the MOF hybrids (Figure 3g). Besides, the synergistic effect between Ni and Fe sites in MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ allows for precise modulation of the electronic structure of Fe sites, resulting in Fe with a valence state of $+(3+\delta)$ for superior OER activity. The Fourier transforms of Ni and Fe $R$ -space data of the extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) insight into the coordination of the metal species. The preponderant peak at $1.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in the Ni $R\mathrm{.}$ -space is ascribed to $\mathsf{N i-O}$ coordination, and the subordinate peak belongs to Ni─Ni(Fe) (Figure S21a, Supporting Information).[7,22] Furthermore, the dominant peak at $\bar{1.56\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ is assigned to the nearest-shell coordination of Fe─O, and the relatively weak peak is referred to Fe─Fe(Ni) (Figure S21b, Supporting Information).[8,21]
## 2.2. Electrocatalytic OER Performance of Electrocatalysts
The electrocatalytic OER performance of the as-prepared electrodes was investigated at a scan rate of $2\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ in a typical three-electrode configuration, using $1.0\ \mathrm{M}$ KOH electrolyte at room temperature. As illustrated in Figure 4a, the series of MOF $\operatorname{s@NFF}$ exhibit the highest OER activity, followed by MOFs $@\mathrm{NF}$ and $\mathsf{M O F s@F F}$ , respectively. These results underscore the pivotal role of partial charge transfer from Fe to Ni in enhancing the OER electrocatalytic performance. Particularly, within the MOFs $@$ NFF series, MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ stands out by achieving an ultralow overpotential of $198~\mathrm{mV}$ to attain a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , outperforming the MOF$274@\mathrm{NFF}$ $(\eta_{10}=234~\mathrm{mV}$ ), $\mathsf{M O F-}74@\mathsf{N F F}$ $(\eta_{10}=249\mathrm{mV}_{.}$ ), and even $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}@\mathrm{NFF}$ $(\eta_{10}=279\:\mathrm{mV}_{\:}$ ). Compared with recent publications on NiFe-based catalysts in 1.0 M KOH solution, MOF- $^{(74+}$ 274) $\boldsymbol{\@}\textcircled{a}\mathrm{NFF}$ exhibits superior performance (Table S4, Supporting Information). Furthermore, the superior catalytic performance of MOF-on-MOF hybrid composites is also observed in MOFs@NF and MOFs $@$ FF series. At the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , the overpotentials of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NF}$ $(\eta_{10}=250\ \mathrm{mV})$ are notably 24 and $49~\mathrm{mV}$ less than those of $\mathsf{M O F-}274@\mathsf{N F}$ and $\mathsf{M O F-}74@\mathsf{N F}$ , respectively. MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{FF}$ with an $\eta_{10}$ of $313\mathrm{mV}$ is lower than those of $\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}274@\mathrm{FF}$ $(\eta_{10}=323\:\mathrm{mV})$ and $\mathrm{MOF-}74@\mathrm{FF}$ $(\eta_{10}=331\mathrm{mV})$ ), highlighting the synergistic effect of MOF hybrids in MOF- $(74+274)$ for significantly enhancing OER performance (Figure 4b). Moreover, MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ features a smaller Tafel slope of $38.3~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ , which is lower than all the other tested catalytic materials, indicating a faster reaction kinetic of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ compared to other MOF catalysts (Figure 4c). Furthermore, the electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) of these materials was estimated from the double-layer capacitance $(C_{\mathrm{dl}})$ by measuring the cyclic voltammetry in a non-Faradaic region (Figure S22, Supporting Information). The MOFs $@\mathrm{FF}$ series hold the highest $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ values than the $\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf\mathrm{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf\mathrm{\Omega}\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf\mathrm{\Omega\mathrm{\Omega}\mathrm{\Omega}\mathsf\mathrm{\Omega\Omega}\mathsf\mathrm{\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm{\Omega}\mathrm\Omega\mathrm{\Omega\Omega\mathrm\Omega\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm{\Omega}\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm{\Omega\Omega\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm{\Omega\Omega\Omega\mathrm\Omega\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\Omega\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm$ and $\mathsf{M O F s@N F}$ series owing to the superior ECSA of the Fe-based electrocatalysts (Figure S23a, Supporting Information).[23] Interestingly, the $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ values of MOF- $(74+274)$ on metal foams (NFF, NF, and FF) are larger than those of single MOF catalysts on corresponding substrates. For example, the $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ is $3.81\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is 1.08 and 1.34 times larger than that of MOF-274 $@$ NFF and MOF-74@NFF, respectively (Figure S23b, Supporting Information). These results suggest the self-supported MOF-on-MOF hybrids could generate abundant active sites, benefiting mass transport and charge transfer, ultimately endowing them with superior OER activity.
The comparison of OER activity of all catalysts was observed by the ECSA-normalized LSV curves (Figure S24a, Supporting Information). The MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ exhibits superior OER activity, requiring an ultralow overpotential of $239\mathrm{mV}$ at the current density of 1 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\mathrm{_{ECSA}}$ , compared to MOF$274@\mathrm{NFF}$ $(274~\mathrm{mV})$ and MOF-74@NFF $(283~\mathrm{mV})$ . This trend is also observed in the $\mathsf{M O F s@N F}$ and MOFs@FF series. Besides, sorting the intrinsic activity of the catalysts follows the order: $\mathrm{MOFs@NFF>MOFs@NF>MOFs@FF}$ . These results further emphasize the synergistic effect of MOF-274 and MOF74, as well as the coupling effect of Ni and Fe ions, in effectively enhancing the electrocatalytic performance. Furthermore, the turnover frequency (TOF) of $0.045{\mathrm{~s}}^{-1}$ at the overpotential of $250\mathrm{mV}$ belongs to MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ , which is higher than that of other catalysts (Figure S24b, Supporting Information). To better understand the significance of the formation of MOF-onMOF hybrids in the OER process, the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was characterized and the Nyquist plots were presented in Figure S25a,b (Supporting Information). Among all MOF catalysts, MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ exhibits the smallest semicircular diameter, indicating its lowest interfacial barrier for mass-transfer and charge-transfer kinetics.[24] In addition, the MOF-on-MOF hybrids display lower charge-transfer resistances $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ than the single MOF, indicating that the unique structure of MOF-on-MOF hybrids can reduce the resistance of electron transfer. The durability of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ was evaluated via chronopotentiometry at a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ in the presence of a $1.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH electrolyte. Impressively, the overpotential of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ only increases $12~\mathrm{mV}$ after $120\mathrm{h}$ of the durability test, while the overpotential for $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}@\mathrm{NFF}$ increases $190~\mathrm{mV}$ (Figure $^{4\mathrm{g})}$ . To further verify the enhanced OER efficiency conferred by the MOF-on-MOF hybrids, we calculated the activation energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ of MOFs@NFF.
The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ can be calculated using the Arrhenius equation.[25]
$$
E_{\mathrm{a}}=R T^{2}\left({\frac{\mathrm{d}{\ln}k}{\mathrm{dT}}}\right)
$$
after integration
$$
\ln k=\mathrm{\Omega}-{\frac{E_{\mathrm{a}}}{R T}}\mathrm{\Omega}+\ln A
$$
where $R$ is the gas constant, $T$ is the absolute temperature, and $k$ is the reaction rate constant measured from Equation (3)
$$
{\frac{i}{n F A}}=k[\mathrm{OH}^{-}]^{x}
$$
where $F$ is the Faraday constant, $i/A$ is the current density, $n$ is the number of electron transfers, and $x$ is the order of the reaction.
As shown in Figure S26 (Supporting Information), the $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values can be determined from the slope $\operatorname{ofln}(j)$ versus $1/T$ plots. Remarkably, MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ exhibits the smallest $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value of $43.2\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ at $1.8\mathrm{V}_{:}$ , which is considerably lower than those of MOF-274@NFF $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $56.4\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ ) and MOF-74 $@$ NFF ( $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $64.6~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ ), confirming that the construction of MOF-onMOF hybrids can significantly expedite OER and enhance electrocatalytic activity.[26]
Next, we conducted a comparative assessment of the OER activities of these materials in $0.1\mathrm{M}$ KOH solution. Similar to OER electrocatalytic performance in $1.0\mathrm{MKOH}$ , the LSV curves reveal that the creation of MOF-on-MOF hybrids can significantly enhance the OER activity. Moreover, the synergistic effect between Ni and Fe proves pivotal in elevating electrocatalytic performance (Figure 4d,e). Specifically, MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ exhibits a superior OER electrocatalytic performance, affording an overpotential of $223~\mathrm{mV}$ at the current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , which surpasses that of most reported NiFe-based catalysts in the $0.1~\mathrm{M}$ KOH electrolyte (Table S5, Supporting Information). The Tafel slope of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ $(72.1~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}},$ ) is lower than that of other MOF catalysts, indicative of its higher catalytic kinetic (Figure 4f). Additionally, the Nyquist plots shown in Figure S25c,d (Supporting Information) demonstrate a decrease in $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ of MOF- $(74+274)$ compared to initial MOF-274, along with the grafting growth of MOF-74 on the MOF-274 substrate. Moreover, the series of MOFs $@$ NFF show the smallest $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ among the materials evaluated, implying that the combination of Ni and Fe species substantially reduces charge-transfer resistance at the catalyst/electrolyte interface. Furthermore, the $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ values of MOFon-MOF hybrids exceed those of single MOF configurations. For instance, MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ presents the highest $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ value of $2.97\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ in the MOFs $@$ NFF series (Figures S23c,d and S27, Supporting Information). In a durability test, involving $120\mathrm{~h~}$ of chronopotentiometry, MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ displays remarkable stability, with only a $9\mathrm{mV}$ decrease in overpotential (Figure $\scriptstyle4\mathrm{g}\mid$ . This underscores its robust performance during the OER test in a 0.1 M KOH solution. However, the overpotential for $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}@\mathrm{NFF}$ increases distinctly over the same testing period. Collectively, these results further affirm the synergistic effects originating from multimetallic centers and the unique MOF-on-MOF heterogeneous architectures, positioning MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ with superior performance in the OER process.
The morphologies and surface element states of MOF- $(74+$ 274) $\boldsymbol{\@}\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{W}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{F}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{F}}$ were characterized by SEM, TEM, and XPS, after the OER process using $1.0\ \mathrm{M}$ KOH as an electrolyte. Remarkably, MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ preserves its inherent rod-like structure after the OER test (Figure $\mathtt{S28a\mathrm{-}d}$ , Supporting Information). Furthermore, as shown in Figure S28e (Supporting Information), the rod-like surface of the MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ retains its distinctive roughness. The elemental mapping confirms the existence of Ni, Fe, C, and O in the MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ rod (Figure S28f, Supporting Information). The XPS survey spectra of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ reveal the continued presence of Ni, Fe, O, and C (Figure S29, Supporting Information). It is noteworthy that two new peaks appear in the $\mathtt{N i}2\mathtt{p}$ spectrum, attributable to $\mathrm{Ni^{3+}}$ , confirming the generation of NiOOH during the OER testing (Figure S30c, Supporting Information). Similarly, in the high-resolution Fe 2p spectrum, the peak area of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ increases while the integral area of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ decreases, compared to the pristine MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ , thus pointing to the generation of FeOOH during testing (Figure S30d, Supporting Information). Furthermore, in the region of O 1s, aside from the peaks ascribed to $_{\mathrm{O-M}}$ , $scriptstyle{\mathrm{O=C-O}}$ , and $\mathtt{C-O}$ , there is a small peak at $853.1\mathrm{eV}$ attributed to MOOH, suggesting that minor local metal ions of MOFs underwent transformation into oxyhydroxide to generation MOFs/MOOH during the OER process (Figure S30b, Supporting Information).[27] Furthermore, the XANES studies were utilized to check the electronic changes of Ni and Fe ions for MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ after the OER test. It is noteworthy that the Ni and Fe $K$ -edges for MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ shift to higher energies after the OER process, verifying the increase of the valence states of Ni and Fe ions, in consistence with the XPS results (Figure S31, Supporting Information). To investigate the structural changes of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ during the OER process, in situ Raman technology was employed. As displayed in Figure S32 (Supporting Information), for the pristine MOF$(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ , the peaks at 1610 and $1308\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are assigned to the stretching vibrations and ring deformation vibrations for the benzene ring of ligands and the stretching vibration of $\mathtt{C-O}$ , respectively. These characteristics persist throughout the electrocatalytic tests conducted at various applied voltages.[28] These results indicate that the MOF structure is stable in the electrocatalytic process. When the potential is set to $1.45\mathrm{V},$ two characteristic peaks appear at 465 and $566~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , which are assigned to the MOOH species.[29] The appearance of two new peaks indicates the gradual formation of MOOH species during the OER process.
## 2.3. Theoretical Calculations
To uncover the effect of MOF-on-MOF-based heterogeneous hybrids in OER performance, density functional theory (DFT) calculations were employed to predict the electronic, structure properties and catalytic mechanism. Figure S33 (Supporting Information) shows the optimized models of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ , MOF-274@NFF, and MOF-74 $@$ NFF. In the case of MOF- $(74+$ 274)@NFF, the coordination unsaturated Fe sites on the surface of MOF-274 were coordinated with the $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ to construct a MOF-on-MOF heterogeneous architecture. First, the charge density difference analyses were simulated, to investigate the electronic structure of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ and its counterparts. Figure 5d shows the presence of charge transfer from MOF-274 to MOF-74 at the MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ interface, signifying the electronic reconstruction within MOF hybrids. The charge accumulation of Ni and charge depletion of Fe are exhibited in Figure 5d and Figure S34 (Supporting Information), confirming the occurrence of charge transfer from Fe to Ni. The results of electronic distribution align with the findings from the XANES and XPS analyses, implying that the electronic characteristic of bimetallic MOF hybrid can be finely tuned through the synergistic effect of these two MOFs and bimetallic sites. To delve deeper into the charges in the electronic structure of electrocatalysts, the density of states (DOS) was employed. As shown in Figure 5b, the local electron states for MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ , MOF-274@NFF, and MOF-74 $@$ NFF all exhibit zero bandgap near the Fermi level, displaying their metallic features. Notably, the intensity of DOS at the adsorption site near Fermi level about MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ surpasses that of $\mathsf{M O F-}274@\mathsf{N F F}$ and $\mathrm{MOF-}74@\mathrm{NFF}$ , underscoring the enhanced conductivity of MOF hybrids compared to individual MOFs, which is in consistence with the EIS results. In addition, the significant upward shift of Fermi level in MOF$(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ indicates that the electron donation from active sites to oxygen-containing intermediates is facilitated, which optimizes the adsorption capability of active sites, contributing to the outstanding OER performance.[30] The d-band center $\left(E_{\mathrm{d}}\right)$ can reflect the absorption ability of intermediates on the catalytic surface, which is presented in Figure 5c. The results reveal that the $E_{\mathrm{d}}$ of Ni 3d in MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ is $-1.133\mathrm{eV},$ in closer proximity to Fermi level compared to MOF-274 $@$ NFF $(-1.557\ \mathrm{eV})$ and MOF-74 $@$ NFF $(-2.011\ \mathrm{eV})$ , corresponding to the stronger binding energy for oxygen intermediates.[31]
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, an in situ two-step solvothermal process was rationally designed for the fabrication of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ , core–shell-like self-supporting MOF hybrids grown on NiFe alloy foam. The MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ shows exceptional catalytic activity toward OER in both 1.0 and $0.1\ensuremath{\mathrm{M}}$ KOH solutions $(\eta_{10}=198$ and $223\mathrm{mV}$ , Tafel slopes of 38.3 and $72.1\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ , respectively). The XPS and XAS results reveal that the electron distribution can be precisely regulated through partial charge transfer between the MOF-274 and MOF-74 upon hybridization. Additionally, the synergistic effect between Ni and Fe species is a significant factor in enhancing OER performance. The DFT calculations indicate the optimized structure of MOF- $(74+274)@\mathrm{NFF}$ can reduce energy barrier of RDS, offering high OER performance. Moreover, the $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value of MOF-on-MOF hybrids is lower than that of single MOFs, facilitating more favorable OER energies and further improving the OER performance. This study not only opens up a new avenue for the development of MOF-based catalysts in the electrocatalytic field, but also provides profound insights into the mechanistic understanding of bimetal MOF hybrids during the OER process.
